          "summary": "[[Fabric Atlas Logo]\nFabric Atlas\n][1]\n[Fibers][2][Fabrics][3][Identify][4][About][5]\n\n# [Fabric Atlas Logo]Fabric Atlas\n\nYour comprehensive guide to textiles and fabrics\n\nDiscover the properties, uses, and possibilities of every fabric imaginable.\nFrom cotton to high-tech synthetics, find the perfect textile for your next project.\n\n## Fabric Identifier\n\nIf you're struggling to identify a piece of fabric you have, our AI-powered fabric identifier can\nhelp. Simply upload a photo and get instant results with confidence levels and detailed fabric\ninformation.\n\n[Try Fabric Identifier →][6]\n\n## Explore Fibers\n\nDiscover the building blocks of textiles. From natural cotton to innovative synthetics, each fiber\nbrings unique properties to fabric creation.\n\n[\n\n### Cashmere\n\nLuxurious ultra-soft fiber from the undercoat of cashmere goats, prized for exceptional warmth and\nsoftness.\n\nnatural animalproteinBiodegradable\nLearn more →\n][7][\n\n### Cotton\n\nThe world's most widely used natural plant fiber, known for breathability, softness, and\nversatility.\n\nnatural plantcellulosicBiodegradable\nLearn more →\n][8][\n\n### Nylon\n\nHigh-strength synthetic polymer fiber known for exceptional durability, elasticity, and abrasion\nresistance.\n\nsyntheticthermoplastic\nLearn more →\n][9][\n\n### Viscose\n\nRegenerated cellulose fiber made from wood pulp, offering cotton-like comfort with silk-like drape.\n\nregenerated cellulosicregeneratedBiodegradable\nLearn more →\n][10][\n\n### Hemp\n\nStrong, durable plant fiber with excellent UV resistance and antimicrobial properties.\n\nnatural plantcellulosicBiodegradable\nLearn more →\n][11][\n\n### PLA\n\nBiodegradable biosynthetic fiber made from renewable plant resources like corn starch.\n\nbio basedthermoplasticBiodegradable\nLearn more →\n][12]\n[View All Fibers][13]\n\n## Explore Fabrics\n\nFrom luxurious silks to sturdy canvas, discover the world of fabrics. Each textile brings its own\ncharacter, drape, and possibilities to your projects.\n\n[\n\n### Faille\n\nFaille is a luxurious woven fabric characterized by its subtle ribbed texture and soft drape,\ncommonly made from silk or polyester.\n\nwovensatin/sateen\nLearn more →Silk, Polyester\n][14][\n\n### Faux Fur\n\nFaux fur is a luxurious pile fabric designed to mimic the look and feel of real animal fur, made\nprimarily from synthetic fibers such as polyester and acrylic.\n\npile nappedpilecut\nLearn more →Polyester, Acrylic+\n][15][\n\n### Felt-Backed Fabric\n\nFelt-backed fabric is a durable laminate material combining a top layer of synthetic fibers with a\nsoft felt backing, offering both structural integrity and comfort.\n\nlaminate coatedbonded\nLearn more →Polyester, Rayon\n][16][\n\n### Filter Cloth\n\nFilter cloth is a laminated fabric primarily made from polyester and polypropylene, designed for\nfiltration applications due to its excellent permeability and durability.\n\nlaminate coatedlaminated\nLearn more →Polyester, Polypropylene\n][17][\n\n### Fishnet\n\nFishnet is a lightweight, open-weave fabric characterized by its net-like structure, commonly made\nfrom nylon or polyester, ideal for decorative and functional applications.\n\nlace netlace/net\nLearn more →Nylon, Polyester\n][18][\n\n### Flannel\n\nFlannel is a soft, napped fabric typically made from cotton or wool, known for its warmth and\ncomfort, making it ideal for cozy garments and bedding.\n\npile nappedbrushed/napped\nLearn more →Cotton, Wool\n][19]\n[View All Fabrics][20]\n\n## Textile Terms\n\nMaster the language of textiles. From basic terminology to technical jargon, build your textile\nvocabulary with our comprehensive glossary.\n\n### ESG\n\n(See also Environmental, Social and Governance\n\nProperties\n\n### EUROCOTON\n\nCommittee of the Cotton and Allied Textile Industries of the European Communities.\n\nFibers\n\n### Earth Pledge\n\nFounded in 1991 in New York City, Earth Pledge Foundation collaborates with government, businesses,\nand communities to advance the adoption of sustainable practices. FutureFashion, one of several\ncompany initiatives, serves to demonstrate that fashion can be sustainable. They are currently\nworking with industry and the public to promote the use of renewable, reusable, and non-polluting\nmaterials and methods.\n\nSustainability\n\n### Eco\n\nOf or relating to habitat or household, mostly used as a prefix related to ecology. Eco comes from\nthe ancient Greek word \"oikos\" (house). e.g. eco-label, eco-friendly, eco-shopping. Within the\ntextile industry, \"eco\" refers to fibers/fabrics that are sustainable or friendly to the\nenvironment.\n\nFibers\n\n### Edge\n\nThe front margin of the garment that extends from front corner to front corner.\n\nMeasurements\n\n### Edge Tape\n\nA tape sewn along the front edge of a coat from top of the lapel to bottom of the facing. On less\nexpensive coats, this tape starts at the bottom of the lapel (called the breakline). The tape is\nusually sewn with an edge-knife machine.\n\nConstruction\n[Browse Full Glossary][21]\n\n### Fabric Atlas\n\nYour comprehensive guide to textiles and fabrics. Discover the properties, uses, and possibil"
