th government, businesses,\nand communities to advance the adoption of sustainable practices. FutureFashion, one of several\ncompany initiatives, serves to demonstrate that fashion can be sustainable. They are currently\nworking with industry and the public to promote the use of renewable, reusable, and non-polluting\nmaterials and methods.\n\nSustainability\n\n### Eco\n\nOf or relating to habitat or household, mostly used as a prefix related to ecology. Eco comes from\nthe ancient Greek word \"oikos\" (house). e.g. eco-label, eco-friendly, eco-shopping. Within the\ntextile industry, \"eco\" refers to fibers/fabrics that are sustainable or friendly to the\nenvironment.\n\nFibers\n\n### Edge\n\nThe front margin of the garment that extends from front corner to front corner.\n\nMeasurements\n\n### Edge Tape\n\nA tape sewn along the front edge of a coat from top of the lapel to bottom of the facing. On less\nexpensive coats, this tape starts at the bottom of the lapel (called the breakline). The tape is\nusually sewn with an edge-knife machine.\n\nConstruction\n[Browse Full Glossary][21]\n\n### Fabric Atlas\n\nYour comprehensive guide to textiles and fabrics. Discover the properties, uses, and possibilities\nof every fabric imaginable.\n\n#### Learn\n\n[All Fabrics][22][All Fibers][23][Identify][24][Glossary][25]\n\n#### Resources\n\n[About Us][26][Contact][27][Sitemap][28]\n\n© 2025 Fabric Atlas. All rights reserved.\n\n[Privacy Policy][29]•[Cookie Policy][30]•[Terms of\nService][31]•[Disclaimer][32]•[Disclosure][33]•[Accessibility][34]\n\n[1]: /\n[2]: /fibers\n[3]: /fabrics\n[4]: /fabric-identifier\n[5]: /about\n[6]: /fabric-identifier\n[7]: /fibers/cashmere\n[8]: /fibers/cotton\n[9]: /fibers/nylon\n[10]: /fibers/viscose\n[11]: /fibers/hemp\n[12]: /fibers/pla\n[13]: /fibers\n[14]: /fabrics/faille\n[15]: /fabrics/faux-fur\n[16]: /fabrics/felt-backed-fabric\n[17]: /fabrics/filter-cloth\n[18]: /fabrics/fishnet\n[19]: /fabrics/flannel\n[20]: /fabrics\n[21]: /glossary\n[22